<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Iberia</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Spain</h1><p class="st-meta">1975 CE – present · capital Madrid</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Spain's present parliamentary monarchy, built through the peaceful transition from Franco's dictatorship to democracy.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Two days after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-franco">Franco</a>'s death, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-carlos-i">Juan Carlos I</a> was proclaimed king on 22 November 1975, ending <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/francoist-spain">Francoist Spain</a>. With Prime Minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adolfo-suarez">Adolfo Suárez</a> he dismantled the dictatorship: the Law for Political Reform of 1976, free elections in June 1977 and the Constitution of 1978, approved by referendum.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> became a parliamentary monarchy divided into 17 autonomous communities, recognising Catalan, Basque and Galician identities. The king's stand against the coup attempt of 23 February 1981 secured democracy. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/felipe-gonzalez">Felipe González's</a> Socialists governed from 1982 to 1996, joining the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-economic-community">European Economic Community</a> in 1986 and keeping Spain in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nato">NATO</a>, which it had joined in 1982, after a referendum that year. The 1992 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/1992-summer-olympics">Barcelona Olympics</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/expo-92">Seville Expo</a> marked modernisation.</p><h2>To the present</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> remains a parliamentary monarchy. ETA declared an end to violence in 2011, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/felipe-vi-of-spain">Felipe VI</a> succeeded his father in 2014, and the Catalan independence crisis of 2017 tested the constitutional order.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-spain.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Spain" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Antonio Valdés y Fernández Bazán · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Kingdom_of_Spain.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1975 CE – present</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Madrid</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Juan Carlos I, Adolfo Suárez, Felipe VI</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Spanish, Catalan, Basque, Galician</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Spain, Reino de España</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=2000&amp;focus=kingdom-of-spain" aria-label="Kingdom of Spain on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4589 733 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Spain lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4909 989L4909 989"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4909 989h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4909 989h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4909 989h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Madrid. <a href="/atlas?year=2000&amp;focus=kingdom-of-spain">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
